    """`retained` plus the transitive dependencies it needs, limited to applied ones.

    A retained change must stay installed, so whatever it depends on must stay
    too. An unapplied dependency is not installed and so is never kept, but the
    walk continues through it: c -> b(unapplied) -> a still keeps a when c is
    retained, because a is installed and c still needs it.

    The walk is iterative because a batch item may carry 2,000+ changes, and a
    dependency chain that deep would exhaust Python's recursion limit. Every
    change is visited at most once -- only a kept ID is pushed -- and the graph
    is acyclic by the time this runs, so two retained IDs whose closures meet
    converge instead of looping.
    """
    keep = set(retained)
    stack = list(retained)
